course: dictionary
lesson: 5185
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning. Context, the words around the word, tells you which meaning the writer wants. «The old house was covered in ivy, its dark shiny leaves hiding the bricks.» Here ivy is a climbing plant, because you cover a house with a plant, not a person.
explain: Look for capital letters too. «Ivy kept saying she'd tell Mum things her big brother hadn't done, unless he gave her his sweets.» Ivy has a capital I and does the telling and the bargaining, so Ivy is a girl's name here.
explain: Check the clues before you decide. «In the USA and Canada, a rash after touching a plant may be poison ivy, so wash with soap and water.» Rash, poison and wash tell you this is a harmful plant, not the harmless climber on the wall. Then swap your meaning into the sentence and see if it still makes sense.
fact: Ivy is an evergreen plant, so its leaves stay green all year.
fact: Ivy climbs walls and trees, holding on with tiny gripping roots.
fact: In winter, ivy berries feed birds, but they are poisonous to people.
fact: «Poison ivy» is a different plant that gives an itchy, blistering rash. The rhyme «Leaves of three, let it be» helps you remember it.
fact: «Ivy» can also be a girl's name, and it starts with a capital letter. The «Ivy League» is a group of old, famous universities in the USA.
exercise: «The old house was covered in ivy, and its dark shiny leaves stayed green all winter.» What does ivy mean here? | *An evergreen climbing plant | A girl's name | A small grey bird | A rash on the skin
exercise: «Ivy kept saying she'd tell Mum things her big brother hadn't done.» Is Ivy a plant or a person? | A rash | A plant that climbs walls | A group of universities | *A person, a girl's name
exercise: «A rash after touching a plant may be poison ivy, so wash with soap and water.» What does poison ivy cause? | Tiny gripping roots | *An itchy, blistering rash | Sweet berries for birds | Dark shiny leaves that stay green
test: «Pea plants climb with tendrils, but ivy climbs with tiny gripping roots.» How does ivy climb? | Wings | Long, thick stems | Tendrils that wrap round things | *Tiny gripping roots
test: At Christmas, «The Holly and the Ivy» is a what? | A university | A poison | *A carol | A rash
exam: Lily reads that «Ivy League» is a famous group of universities. Which clue tells her it is not a plant? | The word Christmas | *The words League and universities | The word green | The word Ivy
